WEBVTT

00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:02.998
What letter is this tree?

00:00:03.848 --> 00:00:09.672
One branch on the right selects the first word of the
old Arabic alphabet mnemonic.

00:00:10.052 --> 00:00:12.975
One on the left selects its first letter.

00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:17.086
Alif. A reader wrote it above the fork in red.

00:00:18.286 --> 00:00:23.235
Try its neighbour. Three branches on the right. Three
on the left. Which letter?

00:00:25.835 --> 00:00:35.282
Ya. The third letter of the third word. Seventy-eight
of the seventy-nine trees agree with their red glosses.
The remaining crown is damaged.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.848
A Mamluk secretary kept these letters from his
colleagues.

00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:49.565
The exchanges are fourteenth-century. Penn dates this
copy to the fifteenth.

00:00:49.765 --> 00:00:54.905
A colleague sent a challenge. The secretary copied it,
then wrote out his reading.

00:00:55.155 --> 00:01:06.397
Penn describes about a hundred and fifty alphabets. The
project’s tables cover a hundred and ninety-nine
ciphers, with a hundred and sixty-one consistent letter
keys.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.546
“My book,” he writes, naming a treatise on ciphers.

00:01:10.816 --> 00:01:14.613
Elsewhere he defends his ancestor’s name: al-Durayhim.

00:01:14.963 --> 00:01:22.406
Together, those clues point to its author, Taj al-Din
Ibn al-Durayhim. A probable identification.

00:01:23.233 --> 00:01:31.489
Another correspondent, al-Jawlani, sent a riddle. On
folio forty-eight, its alphabet survives without an
answer beside it.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:38.763
The project recovered an Arabic riddle. Compare its
cipher with the compiler’s reading earlier in the book.

00:01:39.163 --> 00:01:40.581
Soft of sides.

00:01:41.881 --> 00:01:43.366
It comes at night.

00:01:44.666 --> 00:01:46.116
And leaves at dawn.

00:01:47.116 --> 00:01:48.652
What is the visitor?

00:01:51.102 --> 00:01:56.364
The compiler points to a verse of the Quran: the raised
couches of Paradise.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:04.671
On the last surviving leaf, five red letters start the
reading. Then one set of signs returns three times.

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:09.635
Watch the four signs for the core of this word. Ajfan.
Eyelids.

00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:16.655
The rhythm of Arabic verse helps recover a love poem.
One damaged word still resists a reading.

00:02:17.617 --> 00:02:24.465
Many a girl with drowsy eyelids: love has made my heart
[her] dwelling, and my cheek the ground beneath her.

00:02:25.115 --> 00:02:33.235
Many a girl with drowsy eyelids: love has made my heart
[her] dwelling, and my cheek the ground beneath her.

00:02:33.855 --> 00:02:41.833
I lay awake, though my eyelids are sound, and did not
sleep. She slept, and did not lie awake, though her
eyelids are sick.

00:02:42.483 --> 00:02:50.313
I lay awake, though my eyelids are sound, and did not
sleep. She slept, and did not lie awake, though her
eyelids are sick.
